{"id":"474c7cd7-56b4-4381-a517-e81d5a5708f2","object":"chat.completion","created":1793847449,"model":"deepseek-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"Now I'll add the config item/factory, extend the formatter, and mark stats messages, then verify.\n\n```python\nprint(read_file('deepinfer/__init__.py')['content'])\n\nr = replace_text_batch('deepinfer/environ.py', [\n    {'old_text': '''        return EnvVar(_init)\n\n\nclass envs:''',\n     'new_text': '''        return EnvVar(_init)\n\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_ge(*, minimum: int) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        \"\"\"Optional int parsed with ``int``; unset stays None, values below ``minimum`` are rejected.\"\"\"\n\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n\n\nclass envs:'''},\n    {'old_text': '''    DEEPINFER_TORCH_PROFILER_DIR = EnvVar.str(default=\"/tmp\")''',\n     'new_text': '''    DEEPINFER_TORCH_PROFILER_DIR = EnvVar.str(default=\"/tmp\")\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_ge(minimum=32)\n    \"\"\"默认日志处理器允许的单条普通诊断消息最大字符数；未设置（None）表示不限长。\n    统计日志通过 keep_full_message 标记保持完整输出。\"\"\"'''},\n], 'fe2c531989c419f0b51b169a07f5ed826dcec7f95c0c5bc3a351805923c4f218')\nprint(r)\n```","reasoning_content":"I have all three files' full reads registered and mechanisms exposed. Now make edits.\n\nEdits:\n\n1. environ.py: add `int_or_none_min` factory or just add helper. Simplest: add a factory method `optional_int_min(*, minimum: int)` returning EnvVar that parses int, requires >= minimum. Must include variable name in error text — the wrapper in __get__ prepends `{self.name}: {e}`. But the requirement says the error message must contain the variable name; wrapper adds it. But it re-raises `type(e)(f\"{self.name}: {e}\")`. For ValueError with one arg that works. So a plain ValueError(\"must be >= 32\") would become \"DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS: must be >= 32\". Good. But to be safe, include name explicitly? We can't know name at factory time. Just rely on wrapper.\n\nAdd:\n\n```python\n    @staticmethod\n    def int_or_none_ge(*, minimum: int = 0) -> EnvVar[int | None]:\n        def _init(v: str | None) -> int | None:\n            if v is None:\n                return None\n            value = int(v)\n            if value < minimum:\n                raise ValueError(f\"{v!r} must be >= {minimum}\")\n            return value\n        return EnvVar(_init)\n```\n\nNote empty string: int(\"\") raises ValueError → wrapped. Good.\n\nAdd config item in envs under Profiling & Debug or a Logging section:\n\n```python\n    # -- Logging --\n    DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS = EnvVar.int_or_none_ge(minimum=32)\n```\n\n2. logger.py: extend NewLineFormatter with max_message_chars keyword-only; truncate message.\n\nImplementation: We need to compute record.getMessage() first and truncate before super().format(). But super().format() calls record.getMessage() itself and sets record.message. Careful: must not mutate record. Approach: compute message = record.getMessage(); if len > budget: build truncated; create a shallow copy of record with msg=truncated, args=() and exc_info/stack_info kept? Requirement: preserve exception traceback and stack_info. So copy.copy(record), set msg to truncated, args=(), then super().format(copy). That keeps exc_info and stack_info on the copy — fine since copy shares them but doesn't modify original. Actually exception text (exc_info) formatting appends to end — that's not part of message budget. Good.\n\nBut careful: logging.Formatter.format sets record.message = record.getMessage() on whatever record passed. Using a copy avoids mutation.\n\nNeed `import copy`? Can use `copy.copy`. Add `import copy` at top, or use logging.makeLogRecord? Simpler: use copy module. Allowed stdlib import.\n\nAlso `_LEVEL_COLORS.get(record.levelno)` with copy — levelno same.\n\nTruncation function:\n\n```python\n_TRUNCATION_MARKER = \"...<omitted>...\"\n_MIN_MESSAGE_CHARS = 32\n\n@staticmethod\ndef _truncate_message(msg: str, max_chars: int) -> str:\n    if len(msg) <= max_chars:\n        return msg\n    marker = _TRUNCATION_MARKER\n    remaining = max_chars - len(marker)\n    if remaining <= 0:\n        return msg[:max_chars]\n    head = (remaining + 1) // 2\n    tail = remaining - head\n    return f\"{msg[:head]}{marker}{msg[len(msg) - tail:] if tail else ''}\"\n```\n\nNote min budget 32 > marker len 15, so remaining>0 always. Check: budget 32, marker 15, remaining 17, head = 9, tail = 8. Matches example.\n\nValidation in __init__:\n\n```python\n        if max_message_chars is not None:\n            if isinstance(max_message_chars, bool) or not isinstance(max_message_chars, int) or max_message_chars < 32:\n                raise ValueError(...)\n```\n\nformat:\n\n```python\n    def format(self, record):\n        if self.max_message_chars is not None and not getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", False) is True:\n            message = record.getMessage()\n            if len(message) > self.max_message_chars:\n                record = copy.copy(record)\n                record.msg = self._truncate_message(message, self.max_message_chars)\n                record.args = ()\n```\n\nCareful with the \"exactly True\" check: `record.keep_full_message is True`. Use `getattr(record, \"keep_full_message\", None) is True`.\n\nThen in _setup_logger: `max_message_chars=envs.DEEPINFER_LOG_MAX_MESSAGE_CHARS`. Wait — importing envs in logger.py — check circular import. environ.py imports only stdlib; logger imports envs → fine. Where is envs imported in logger currently? Not imported. deepinfer/__init__ maybe imports envs. `from deepinfer import envs` in logger should be fine since environ has no dependency on logger. But careful: deepinfer/__init__.py might import logger... `from deepinfer import envs` triggers deepinfer/__init__ execution, which might import logger → circular. Safer: `from deepinfer.environ import envs`? That still runs deepinfer/__init__. Hmm, any import of a submodule executes the package __init__. Let me check deepinfer/__init__.py content — I read it earlier (registered full read). Let me print it from evidence... I don't have its content in this message. I should read it again? Reads are limited maybe; but reading __init__.py again is fine (not required read). Better to check quickly.\n\nAlternative: import inside `_setup_logger` function to avoid circularity at module import time. That's safe: `from deepinfer import envs` inside function. But `_setup_logger()` is called at module import (line `_setup_logger()`), so it still runs at import time. Hmm, same circular risk: when deepinfer/__init__ imports logger, logger's module-level `_setup_logger()` call needs envs, which needs deepinfer/__init__ to be at least partially initialized — importing deepinfer.env... Actually `from deepinfer import envs` during partial initialization of deepinfer package: Python will import deepinfer (already in sys.modules, partial), then try getattr(deepinfer, 'envs'), and since Python 3.7 falls back to importing deepinfer.envs submodule, which works. So it's fine even at module import, as long as environ doesn't import logger.\n\nBut note environ.py imports nothing from deepinfer. Good.\n\nAlso stats_logger imports `from deepinfer import envs` already — so pattern is used.\n\nI'll do the import at top of logger.py: `from deepinfer import envs`. Wait order: logger.py is imported by environ? No. Fine.\n\nHmm, but check `deepinfer/__init__.py` — it may import logger? Registered read of it suggests I looked at it. Let me read it to be safe in this action too (cheap). Actually I can print it via read_file again; but that's fine, doesn't reset anything.\n\nAlso lazy: to be safest, do the import inside `_setup_l